        "text": "Fail-open refactor: `files.readFile(path).catch(() => null)` masks non-ENOENT errors, including authorization failures and transient runtime errors, as absent files. The previous code only treated ENOENT as missing and threw RUN_ACCESS_VIOLATION for other failures.",
        "evidence": {
          "quote": "    // A file the SDK cannot read counts as absent, so a missing login never blocks a run.\n    const response = await files.readFile(path).catch(() => null);\n\n    if (response === null || response.status === 404) {",
          "why": "Any `readFile` rejection other than ENOENT no longer triggers an access-violation error. This changes a fail-closed guard into a fail-open one. The stated intent is to not block runs when a login file was never set up, but the catch-all also treats SDK authorization errors or runtime failures as missing files."
        }
      }
    ],
